WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.965
Imagine a paper that would be useful for

00:00:02.985 --> 00:00:04.227
your particular question.

00:00:04.389 --> 00:00:06.650
The first retrieval stage misses it.

00:00:06.799 --> 00:00:09.150
Now a much stronger model reranks the

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:11.895
shortlist. Can that model put the missing

00:00:11.915 --> 00:00:14.743
paper first? Pause and predict what

00:00:14.763 --> 00:00:16.778
information it actually receives.

00:00:19.174 --> 00:00:21.894
It receives the shortlisted candidates.

00:00:22.044 --> 00:00:24.575
Reranking can change their order, but the

00:00:24.595 --> 00:00:26.971
absent paper is unavailable.

00:00:27.134 --> 00:00:29.936
Our invented cards illustrate a boundary

00:00:29.956 --> 00:00:32.023
inherited by later stages.

00:00:32.922 --> 00:00:34.745
Imagine millions of records.

00:00:34.908 --> 00:00:37.762
Applying an expensive query and document

00:00:37.782 --> 00:00:40.481
comparison to every record for every

00:00:40.501 --> 00:00:42.903
search would usually cost too much.

00:00:43.129 --> 00:00:45.764
Fast first stages retrieve a manageable

00:00:45.784 --> 00:00:49.179
set; later stages spend more computation

00:00:49.199 --> 00:00:50.570
on each survivor.

00:00:51.118 --> 00:00:53.410
The first stage balances speed and

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.755
retaining potentially relevant records.

00:00:55.917 --> 00:00:58.307
Reranking aims to improve the ordering

00:00:58.327 --> 00:00:59.479
within its pool.

00:00:59.628 --> 00:01:02.224
A later answer generator inherits that

00:01:02.244 --> 00:01:04.995
candidate boundary too, unless another

00:01:05.015 --> 00:01:07.804
operation explicitly brings in additional

00:01:07.824 --> 00:01:08.667
evidence.

00:01:09.568 --> 00:01:11.735
A conventional bi encoder represents the

00:01:11.755 --> 00:01:14.762
query and passage independently.

00:01:14.938 --> 00:01:17.276
Passage vectors can be stored before any

00:01:17.296 --> 00:01:20.523
query arrives. Their interaction happens

00:01:20.543 --> 00:01:22.971
after each text has been compressed.

00:01:23.134 --> 00:01:25.446
A cross encoder instead processes the

00:01:25.466 --> 00:01:28.190
query and one candidate together.

00:01:28.742 --> 00:01:31.113
Attention can run across their tokens

00:01:31.134 --> 00:01:33.716
before producing a ranking score.

00:01:33.879 --> 00:01:35.972
That creates an opportunity to examine a

00:01:35.992 --> 00:01:39.954
qualifier, negation, entity or intent more

00:01:39.974 --> 00:01:42.660
closely. Cross encoder names an

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.980
arrangement; the score still reflects

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.711
training and the chosen relevance task.

00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:51.297
The chapter’s query asks: am I being

00:01:51.317 --> 00:01:53.229
delulu about getting this job?

00:01:53.405 --> 00:01:56.065
One candidate explains how to recognise

00:01:56.085 --> 00:01:58.501
unrealistic expectations during a job

00:01:58.521 --> 00:02:01.555
search. Another lists ten signs that you

00:02:01.575 --> 00:02:03.784
performed well in your job interview.

00:02:04.348 --> 00:02:06.960
Both concern job seeking and advice.

00:02:07.161 --> 00:02:09.165
For the stated need about unrealistic

00:02:09.185 --> 00:02:12.167
expectations, the first is the intended

00:02:12.187 --> 00:02:15.144
match; interview performance is the hard

00:02:15.164 --> 00:02:17.889
negative. Joint query and candidate

00:02:17.909 --> 00:02:20.930
processing exposes details that a pooled

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:22.824
representation may blur.

00:02:23.724 --> 00:02:25.287
The cross encoder’s candidate

00:02:25.307 --> 00:02:27.348
representation depends on the incoming

00:02:27.368 --> 00:02:29.874
query, so it cannot be precomputed and

00:02:29.894 --> 00:02:31.755
reused in the same way.

00:02:31.905 --> 00:02:34.423
Reranking a hundred candidates requires

00:02:34.443 --> 00:02:37.207
roughly a hundred query and candidate

00:02:37.227 --> 00:02:40.080
evaluations, even when implementation

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:41.317
batches the work.

00:02:41.856 --> 00:02:43.819
That count is not a measured latency

00:02:43.839 --> 00:02:46.461
estimate. Truncation can also hide

00:02:46.481 --> 00:02:48.897
decisive text. A richer scoring

00:02:48.917 --> 00:02:50.765
arrangement makes more evidence

00:02:50.785 --> 00:02:52.969
accessible; it does not establish

00:02:52.989 --> 00:02:55.455
objective or infallible relevance.

00:02:56.364 --> 00:02:59.459
An LLM can receive the query, candidate

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:02.036
text and explicit criteria such as

00:03:02.056 --> 00:03:04.678
population, study design or whether a

00:03:04.698 --> 00:03:06.391
passage answers the question.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:09.201
Pointwise prompting judges each candidate

00:03:09.221 --> 00:03:12.333
separately. Pairwise prompting asks which

00:03:12.353 --> 00:03:14.793
of two candidates is more relevant.

00:03:15.342 --> 00:03:17.555
Listwise prompting presents several

00:03:17.575 --> 00:03:19.874
candidates together and requests an

00:03:19.894 --> 00:03:23.057
ordering. Larger lists may require windows

00:03:23.077 --> 00:03:25.131
or repeated partial rankings.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.915
Here these names describe prompting,

00:03:28.039 --> 00:03:30.132
rather than classical learning to rank

00:03:30.152 --> 00:03:31.639
training objectives.

00:03:32.539 --> 00:03:34.742
Each arrangement has failure modes.

00:03:34.904 --> 00:03:37.705
Separate scores may need calibration.

00:03:37.868 --> 00:03:40.515
Pairwise preferences can be inconsistent.

00:03:40.677 --> 00:03:42.642
Listwise outputs may depend on input

00:03:42.662 --> 00:03:46.044
order. Prompt wording, context limits,

00:03:46.064 --> 00:03:48.750
model updates and sampling settings can

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:51.288
all affect an LLM reranker.

00:03:51.828 --> 00:03:53.662
A fluent explanation can help someone

00:03:53.682 --> 00:03:56.369
inspect a decision, but it is not evidence

00:03:56.389 --> 00:03:58.262
that the decision was correct.

00:03:58.412 --> 00:04:01.240
Evaluate the complete pipeline against the

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:03.611
actual information need, including

00:04:03.631 --> 00:04:06.098
candidates that an earlier stage failed to

00:04:06.118 --> 00:04:07.012
supply.

00:04:07.949 --> 00:04:10.104
The chapter’s documented Semantic Scholar

00:04:10.124 --> 00:04:12.089
account from twenty twenty five uses

00:04:12.109 --> 00:04:14.576
Elasticsearch keyword retrieval over

00:04:14.596 --> 00:04:18.016
titles, abstracts and author names.

00:04:18.166 --> 00:04:20.620
Up to a thousand candidates then reach a

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.396
LightGBM reranker using signals including

00:04:24.416 --> 00:04:27.823
title matches, citations and recency.

00:04:28.378 --> 00:04:30.697
That is machine learnt ranking after

00:04:30.717 --> 00:04:32.062
lexical retrieval.

00:04:32.225 --> 00:04:34.885
It establishes neither dense first stage

00:04:34.905 --> 00:04:38.261
retrieval nor an LLM reranker, and does

00:04:38.281 --> 00:04:40.439
not explain the precise later interface

00:04:40.459 --> 00:04:42.849
behaviour. Ask what produced the pool,

00:04:42.869 --> 00:04:45.246
what text the ranker saw, and how many

00:04:45.266 --> 00:04:46.663
candidates reached it.
